#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void addBonusByValue(int marks) { marks += 5; }          // changes a copy
void addBonusByRef(int& marks) { marks += 5; }           // changes the caller's variable
void addBonusByPtr(int* marks) { if (marks) *marks += 5; }  // same, through an address

int main() {
    int a = 70, b = 70, c = 70;
    addBonusByValue(a);
    addBonusByRef(b);
    addBonusByPtr(&c);
    cout << "value: " << a << ", reference: " << b << ", pointer: " << c << "\n";
    int& alias = b;      // alias is another name for b, forever
    alias = 100;
    cout << "b after alias = 100: " << b << "\n";
    return 0;
}

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

struct Roster {
    vector<string> names;
    static inline int copies = 0;
    Roster() = default;
    Roster(const Roster& other) : names(other.names) { copies++; }   // count every copy
};

size_t countByValue(Roster r) { return r.names.size(); }
size_t countByConstRef(const Roster& r) { return r.names.size(); }

int main() {
    Roster hostel;
    hostel.names = {"Aditi", "Bilal", "Charu"};
    countByValue(hostel);
    countByValue(hostel);
    countByConstRef(hostel);
    countByConstRef(hostel);
    cout << "copies made: " << Roster::copies << "\n";
    return 0;
}

sections.body:
A reference is only valid while the object it names is alive. This function compiles, with a warning:

```cpp
const string& bestHostel() {
    string name = "Kaveri";
    return name;          // name dies here
}
```

The caller receives a reference to a destroyed string. Reading it is **undefined behaviour**: it may print "Kaveri" today and garbage tomorrow. The same applies to returning a pointer to a local variable. Return **by value** instead (`string bestHostel()`): modern C++ moves or elides the copy, so it costs almost nothing.

References to elements of a `vector` have a related trap: if the vector grows past its capacity, it moves its elements to new memory, and every reference or pointer into the old memory dangles. The next lesson covers that in detail.

In the hands-on task you will write a function that must change both of the caller's variables. Try it by value first in your head: the swap would happen to copies and the caller would see nothing. That is exactly the bug a reference parameter fixes.

